= Addendum (July 2026): Verification Campaign and New Results

Between July 19 and July 21, 2026, the results of this paper were subjected to a multi-round adversarial verification campaign. Independent verification passes re-derived Theorems A--D and the Section 6--8 reductions from scratch: the FMF formulas were re-checked parametrically for every $t <= 300$ and exhaustively to $2 times 10^6$; the state-independence counts, the quartering densities, and the full reduction chain (including the constants $2\/15 = sum_r 2^(-(4r+3))$ and $1\/112 = sum_k 2^(-(3k+7))$) were re-implemented independently with zero discrepancies. One numerical error was found and corrected: the critical exponent of the fractional-moment series is $alpha^* = 1$ _exactly_, via the rational identity $M(1) = 1\/4 + 3\/16 + 9\/16 = 1$; the earlier value $1.0002$ was a truncation artifact. Section 4 and Section 9 already incorporate this correction.

The attack rounds of the same campaign produced the new results below. With two exceptions, every theorem stated in this addendum survived two independent adversarial referee passes that re-implemented and attacked each step; the exceptions are the two definitive negative results --- the full-shift ambient-language theorem and the mirror-equivalence/two-prime-vacuity theorem --- which carry short machine-verified proofs but were logged as negative results and not separately refereed. Full statements, proofs, and referee records are in the repository (`proof.md`, Section 23, Theorems 45--62, and the session artifacts under `notes/round4-july2026/` and `notes/round5-july2026/`). This addendum states the results with one-paragraph proof sketches. None of them proves the Collatz conjecture; their combined effect is to sharpen the open pointwise gap of Section 9 and to delimit, by proved negative results, several routes that cannot close it.

== Symbolic exclusions: complexity thresholds and the universal morphic theorem

The endpoint exclusions of Sections 6--8 removed periodic, finite-defect, sparse-label, low-complexity, balanced/Sturmian, and several individual automatic templates. The campaign replaced this list by two uniform theorems.

#theorem(name: "Complexity Threshold")[
  Every infinite ${0,1}$ return word admitting a positive ordinary return code satisfies $liminf_(m arrow.r infinity) p(m)\/m >= T_R$ with $T_R in (3.1493, 3.1494)$; every infinite ${0,1}$ reduced-gap word admitting a positive ordinary gap code satisfies $liminf_(m arrow.r infinity) p(m)\/m >= T_G$ with $T_G in (6.2390, 6.2391)$, where $p$ is the factor complexity function.
]

_Proof sketch._ A Same-Factor State-Separation lemma shows that equal length-$m$ factors with $kappa$ ones occurring at two positions force the corresponding orbit states into the same residue class modulo the standalone factor modulus ($2^(3m+4 kappa)$ in the return system, $2^(7m+3 kappa)$ in the gap system) while strict state growth keeps them distinct. If the complexity $p(m)$ is below the threshold, an occurrence-counting argument produces too many such forced coincidences: the states must outrun the landing growth envelope, a contradiction. This subsumes the eventually-periodic, fixed-defect, sparse-label, low-complexity, and balanced/Sturmian exclusions of Sections 6--8 in one stroke, and applies to non-recurrent words that prefix-recurrence machinery cannot touch. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 47.

#theorem(name: "Universal Uniform-Morphic Exclusion, Theorem G")[
  Let $y in {0,1}^infinity$ be a fixed point of any $q$-uniform morphism $phi: {0,1} arrow.r {0,1}^q$, $q >= 2$, with no aperiodicity or primitivity assumption. Then $y$ admits no positive ordinary reduced-gap code: a code $A$ would have to satisfy $A > 2^(3 q^t)$ for every $t >= 0$. In the return system, $M + 1 > 2^(q^t)$. Consequently, fixed points over alphabets of at most $6$ letters (gap) or $3$ letters (return) are excluded under the standard codings, hence every binary $q$-automatic gap word with an msd-first DFAO of at most $6$ states, for every $q$.
]

_Proof sketch._ By pigeonhole, two of the first three letters of $y$ are equal, at positions $p_1 < p_2 <= 2$. Applying $phi^t$ produces the same length-$q^t$ factor at positions $p_1 q^t$ and $p_2 q^t <= 2 q^t$. The gap shift-code recurrence and the forced-prefix-congruence lemma of Section 7 then force the two orbit states to be congruent modulo $2^(7m+3 kappa)$, while strict monotonicity and the growth envelope cap the later state; the contradiction reduces to the integer certificates $3^7 < 2^12$ (gap) and $3^5 < 2^8$ (return). This subsumes the paper's separate $q = 2, dots, 6$ template classifications and the $q = 9$ boundary word below in a single argument. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 51.

#proposition(name: "The q = 9 Boundary Word")[
  The fixed point $w$ of the $9$-uniform morphism $0 arrow.r 011111111$, $1 arrow.r 100000000$ (equivalently, $w_n$ is the parity of the number of nonzero base-$9$ digits of $n$) --- the last uniform-morphic template left open by Section 8 --- has no positive ordinary reduced-gap code and no positive ordinary return code.
]

_Proof sketch._ This was proved first, by a new _factor-recurrence height barrier_ that strictly generalizes the initial-prefix-recurrence barrier of Sections 6--8 to arbitrary (including overlapping) factor occurrences: equal length-$m$ factors with $kappa$ ones at positions $i < i'$ force $a_i equiv a_(i') mod 2^(7m+3 kappa)$ with $a_(i') > a_i$. Applied to the scaled eighth-power factor $B_t^8$ occurring at positions $9^t$ and $2 dot 9^t$ for every $t >= 1$, the modulus $2^(68 dot 9^t + 12 (-7)^t)$ beats the growth envelope $A dot 3^(12 dot 9^t) \/ 2^(17 dot 9^t)$, forcing $A > 2^(55 dot 9^t)$ for all $t$ --- impossible. Secondary results: the initial recurrence function of $w$ satisfies $min_l sigma(l)\/l = 17\/2$ exactly, while the old initial-prefix route needed a value below $8.41772$, so that route was structurally dead (it fails by exactly the Pythagorean comma $3^12\/2^19$); the full recurrence function is $R(n) = 747 dot 9^k + n - 1$ on the bands $[9^k + 2, 9^(k+1) + 1]$, with $R(n) <= 748 n - 1$ sharp. The theorem is now superseded by Theorem G, which re-proves it with one factor copy instead of eight. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 46.

#theorem(name: "Full-Shift Ambient Language and Sharpened 3-Dependent Thresholds")[
  The ambient language of both coding systems is the full shift: every finite binary word occupies exactly one nonempty residue class, and every infinite binary word is the exact itinerary of a unique $2$-adic seed. Hence no upper bound on landing-word complexity can follow from the generation mechanism alone --- a complexity pincer is impossible without the positivity hypothesis itself. The salvage is a sharpened pointwise, density-coupled threshold: return landing words satisfy $p_w (m) >= (3m - log_2 (M+1)) \/ (5 log_2 3 - 7)$, giving $liminf p(m)\/m >= T'_R = 3.2439007$; the gap analogue gives $T'_G = 6.3942268$; both systems share the uniform-density floor $7 \/ (5 log_2 3 - 7) = 7.5691$.
]

_Proof sketch._ The full-shift statement follows from the residue-class structure of the block maps: every letter refines the class and no class is ever empty, so arbitrary words --- including words of maximal complexity $p(m) = 2^m$ --- are realized by $2$-adic seeds. The sharpened thresholds re-run the state-separation counting with the density coupling made explicit. The bound is genuinely $3$-dependent: for the map $a n + 1$ the return threshold becomes $T'_R (a) = 3 \/ (5 log_2 a - 7)$, which is vacuous exactly for $a >= 4$ --- the first complexity-type exclusion that passes the $5n+1$ litmus test of Section 9. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 60, and `notes/round4-july2026/complexity-pincer/`.

== Rank counting: unconditional bounds and exact criticality

Section 1's Uniform Terminal-Window Rank Bound conjecture, developed through Sections 6--7, posed the terminal-window rank bounds $W_R (B,L) <= C_R (B+1)(2\/15)^L$ and $W_G (B,L) <= C_G B thin 112^(-L)$ as the missing counting theorem. The campaign proved the first unconditional unweighted estimates, and then proved that their shape is essentially optimal for a whole class of methods.

#theorem(name: "AP Survivor Bounds")[
  There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, with $D_L$ the set of integers formally admitting at least $L$ binary return blocks, for every arithmetic progression $P$ of odd difference and length $N$: $|P inter D_L| <= C N^(3\/10)$ for all $L >= log_8 N + 3$, and $|P inter D_L| <= C(N 2^(-2.1 L) + 2^(0.9 L))$ for all $L$. In particular $W_R (B,L) <= C((B+1) 2^(-2.1 L) + 2^(0.9 L))$ and $W_G (B,L) <= C(B dot 2^(-5.46 L) + 2^(1.54 L))$. The survivor exponent floors are $alpha_R = 0.287761$ (the root of $sum_r 2^(-(4r+3) beta) = 1$) and $alpha_G = 0.212323$.
]

_Proof sketch._ The first return block confines survivors to an explicit arithmetic-progression decomposition; iterating the decomposition gives a two-term recursion whose main term contracts exponentially with no expansion cutoff, at the cost of the additive $2^(0.9 L)$ defect term counting thick classes. The referee pass re-derived every constant, re-ran the recursion to $N = 10^8$, and brute-forced $W_R$ to $B = 2^18$, $L = 12$. Two companion results delimit the method: (i) _saturation obstruction_ --- translate-invariant counting schemes (second-moment, Cauchy--Schwarz, and large-sieve arguments on the congruence-class structure) can never reach the target shape $C(B+1) theta^L$, because the supremum over translated progressions of length $1$ stays at least $1$ at every $L$; any proof of the full rank bound must use arithmetic separation of normalized terminal heights, confirming Section 6's own diagnosis. (ii) _Downstream sufficiency_ --- the endpoint application does not need the sharp bases $2\/15$ and $1\/112$: any uniform bound $f(B) g(L)$ with $g(L) arrow.r 0$ suffices. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 48.

#theorem(name: "Exact Criticality of the Rank Bases; Class-Counting No-Go")[
  (T1) $W_R (B,L) >= binom(L+s, s)$ with $s$ of order $(log_2 B - 3L)\/4$; hence $sup_L W_R (B,L) >= B^(alpha_R - o(1))$ inside the anchored window: the AP Survivor exponent $alpha_R = 0.287761$ is optimal even for position-aware counting. (T2) For every $rho < 2\/15$ the bound $W_R (B,L) <= C(B+1) rho^L$ is false along $(L,B) = (15s, 2^(49s))$, via the exact identity $16 H(1\/16) = 49 + 15 log_2 (2\/15)$, where $H$ is the binary entropy function; the base $2\/15$ is exactly critical, with floor ratio of order $s^(-1\/2)$ at $rho = 2\/15$. The gap base $1\/112$ is exactly critical via $8 H(1\/8) = 52 - 7 log_2 112$. (E) The per-$B$ decay target ($W_R (B,L) <= C_B g(L)$ with $g arrow.r 0$, for every $B$) is equivalent to $mu_L arrow.r infinity$. (Blocking) Every certificate produced by the interval-plus-progression counting recursion is $>= 1$ for all $(B,L)$, via the $r = 0$ chain (witness: the true count $W_R (100,3) = 0$ is certified only as $>= 1$) --- so no class-counting scheme of this kind can prove $H_R (B) < infinity$ for any $B$.
]

_Proof sketch._ The floor (T1) counts thick words directly inside the anchored window by a stars-and-bars enumeration of admissible depth patterns; the exponent identity $alpha_R = -log_2 y$ with $y^3 + y^4 = 1$ makes the floor match the AP Survivor exponent. The criticality identities (T2) are entropy computations along the extremal family: the binomial mass of the thick classes exactly balances the target decay at $rho = 2\/15$ (resp. $1\/112$), and strictly beats it below. The blocking statement is an exact fixed point of the recursion. All statements transfer verbatim to $5n+1$, as the next theorem demands. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 57.

#theorem(name: "The Rank Conjecture is 3-Free: the 5n+1 Litmus on the Endgame")[
  The entire binary return and reduced-gap counting apparatus transfers verbatim to the $5n+1$ map: the same gate moduli $Q_r = 2^(4r+3)$ and $q_k = 2^(3k+7)$, the same survival bases $2\/15$ and $1\/112$, the same quartering factor $1\/4$, the same terminal-window identities and periodic exclusions, with only the odd-side data changing ($P_r = 25 dot 125^r$, $D_r = (160 dot 125^r - 51 dot 16^r)\/109$). Since $5n+1$ has the nontrivial cycle through $13$, the rank bounds $W_R, W_G$ --- even proved with sharp constants --- cannot by themselves imply convergence.
]

_Proof sketch._ The referee pass re-derived the $a = 5$ block coding from scratch and confirmed that the survival bases are purely $2$-adic: $2\/15 = (1\/8) \/ (1 - 1\/16)$, with $15 = 2^4 - 1$ and not $3 dot 5$; the multiplier only shifts the odd gate residues. The concrete witness is $F_5 (13) = 13$: a genuine $5n+1$ FMF fixed point with $v_2 = 5$, lying outside every excluded symbolic class. Hence the excluded classes are consistent with both convergence and divergence, and the constant $3$ (through $a\/4 < 1$, the deterministic Type-A contraction channel of Section 9) must enter at the mixed growth/recovery completion step --- exactly where the next theorem places it. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 49.

== The two-lemma reduction

The following theorem gives the sharpest known factorization of the remaining pointwise problem in the FMF framework. Write each FMF hop as a letter $l_j = (s_j, v_j)$ with $s = v_2 (x+1)$ and $v = v_2 ("FMF")$, and let $G_(01) = {(2,2), (3,2)}$ be the depth-${0,1}$ growth-B letters.

#theorem(name: "Two-Lemma Reduction")[
  Let H1 be the statement that the binary return partial map $T$ has no infinite positive-integer orbit (equivalently $mu_L arrow.r infinity$), and let Lemma E be the statement that every non-descending FMF orbit is eventually a depth-${0,1}$ growth-B chain (letters outside $G_(01)$ occur only finitely often). Then H1 and Lemma E together imply that every odd $x > 1$ has finite epoch, hence pointwise FMF descent, hence the Collatz conjecture.
]

_Proof sketch._ Lemma E confines any non-descending orbit tail to the depth-${0,1}$ alphabet; the new unconditional *Lemma P* --- the map $m' = (27m+1)\/16$ has no infinite positive orbit, because the exact identity $16(11 m' + 1) = 27(11 m + 1)$ forces $v_2 (11 m_0 + 1) >= 4k$ for all $k$ --- and H1 then exclude the two possible tail regimes, so every orbit descends. Supporting results proved with the reduction: the _ballot inequality_ for non-descending prefixes, $S_N \/ N >= log_(3\/2) 2 approx 1.7095$, whose coefficient $2 - log_2 a$ flips sign exactly at $a = 4$; bounded depth-sum orbits descend effectively; eventually-periodic hop words are exactly cycles, so no divergent orbit has an eventually periodic hop word; and the mixed-pattern shadow theorem (every finite hop word occupies exactly one residue class mod $2^(H+1)$, so no finite forbidden-pattern strategy can work). Lemma E's $5n+1$ analogue is _false_ --- $F_5 (13) = 13$ has hop letter $(3,5)$ --- so E carries all the $3$-dependence of any completion through this framework. A dimension identity calibrates the division of labor: $alpha_R = -log_2 y$ with $y^3 + y^4 = 1$, so the rank program's scope has box dimension $0.2878$, versus at least $0.9470$ for the uncertified Cantor set that Lemma E must empty. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 55.

== Arithmetic structure of the remaining gap

Two structural theorems, from the final round of the campaign, pin down what the remaining hypotheses H1 and Lemma E actually are.

#theorem(name: "Mirror Equivalence and Two-Prime Vacuity")[
  For every finite return word of length $N$, the state $U_N = (P_N R_N + D_N)\/Q_N$ reached by the least $2$-adic prefix residue $R_N$ equals the least nonnegative residue $V_N$ of $D_N Q_N^(-1)$ modulo $P_N = 3^(2N + 3 S_N)$: the $2$-adic seed data and the $3$-adic state data of a prefix are the same information under an explicit affine bijection. Consequently (Theorem V), for fixed $M$, "$3$-adically consistent with positive integer states below the envelope of $M$" is verbatim the rank condition $R_N <= M$; the per-block $3$-adic consistency fraction is exactly $1$, and no two-prime pincer using state residues modulo powers of $3$, positivity, and envelopes can prune the word tree beyond the rank program.
]

_Proof sketch._ The equivalence is a one-line congruence check: $Q_N | P_N R_N + D_N$, $0 < U_N < P_N$, and $U_N Q_N equiv D_N mod P_N$ identify $U_N$ as the least residue. It was machine-verified on 2,184 words (for both $a = 3$ and $a = 5$), and Theorem V exhaustively on 398,480 word-envelope checks with zero violations. A census over $10^6$ seeds shows every kill is a $2$-adic gate failure ($7\/15$ and $6\/15$ per step), with zero positivity kills and zero $3$-adic kills; the whole apparatus is $a$-blind (the $5$-adic mirror for $5n+1$ is identical). The salvage is an exact two-prime information ledger (letter $r$ consumes $4r+3$ seed bits forward and dictates $3r+2$ state trits backward --- the state's low ternary digits spell the reversed recent word) and a new equivalent _mirror form_ of H1: a small-least-residue statement modulo powers of $3$ with $2$-power denominators, placing H1 in the Erdős--Lagarias ternary-digits-of-$2^n$ and Furstenberg $times 2 times 3$ rigidity class. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 61, and `notes/round5-july2026/`.

#theorem(name: "Cocycle, Reset Law, and the Core of Lemma E")[
  (A1) The block data compose by the twisted cocycle $B_(u w) = 3^(S_w) B_u + 2^(H_u) B_w$ (and identically for $D$); Lemma P is the power-degenerate case. (A2) Master identity: $v_2 (D_p x_0 - B_p) = H_p + v_2 (x_0 - x_k)$ for every prefix $p$ of every hop word --- every prefix is an exact divisibility statement about a single integer. (A3) A fixed-form certificate forcing $v_2 (a x_0 + b) arrow.r infinity$ exists if and only if the word's $2$-adic seed $xi(w)$ is rational; for irrational seeds the Lemma-P mechanism provably cannot apply, and recognizing aperiodic rational-seed words is equivalent in difficulty to the Lagarias periodicity conjecture. (B) Reset law: crossing an intervening block $m$ updates the accumulated valuation $nu$ by $nu' = min(nu, omega) - H_m$, where $omega(u,m) = v_2 (D_u B_m - B_u D_m)$ is a Wronskian level given by a proved closed formula; the only escape from resetting is the exact-coincidence channel $nu = omega$.
]

_Proof sketch._ (A1) and (A2) are direct inductions on the affine block maps. (A3) reduces certificate existence to $a xi + b = 0$. The reset law follows from the ultrametric trichotomy for $v_2$ of a sum; the closed formula for $omega$ expresses it through the longest common prefix of the periodizations $u^infinity, m^infinity$ and the first disagreeing letters. The residual core of Lemma E is thereby identified: an infinite non-descending obstruction requires infinitely many exact valuation coincidences --- $2$-adic near-approaches of the orbit to eventually-periodic rational seeds --- with orbit size the only non-circular lever, a $2$-adic Diophantine-approximation statement. A new unconditional slice falls out: the _spike-ledger theorem_ excludes all hop words whose depth spikes outrun the drift budget at geometric ratio exceeding $log_2 3$ (sharp, and multiplier-covariant: for $5n+1$ the boundary moves to $log_2 5$) --- the first proved exclusion inside the open unbounded-alphabet branch of Section 8. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 62, and `notes/round5-july2026/`.

== Computational records

#theorem(name: "Pointwise Descent Record")[
  For every odd $x > 1$ (unbounded) with coefficient stopping time $kappa(x)$ at most 301,993 Terras steps (492,529 single steps): $T^j (x) > x$ for $j < kappa(x)$ and $T^(kappa(x)) (x) < x$, i.e. $sigma(x) = kappa(x)$. Equivalently, for every $n <= $ 301,993, every odd $x > 1$ outside an explicit ballot family $cal(B)_n mod 2^n$ (of size $|cal(B)_n| = $ A076227$(n)$ and density below $2^(-15093)$ at the top horizon) descends below itself within $n$ steps, uniformly in $x$.
]

_Proof sketch._ A proved threshold lemma bounds the possible exceptions at horizon $m$ by an explicit $B(m)$; an exact integer ledger evaluates $Theta(301,993) = $ 10,824,133,905; and a zero-exception scan of $1.35 times 10^9$ candidates closes the frontier. This extends Terras's 1976 horizon (2,593) by a factor 116 and Garner's 1981 conditional horizon (64,508) by 4.7; conditional on Rozier--Terracol (Discrete Math., 2026) it extends to $kappa <= $ 665,684,250. Corollary: every nontrivial cycle has Terras length exceeding 301,993 (the cycle bounds below remain stronger). The structure of the uncertified tree is now exact: ballot characterization, $1$-or-$2$ branching with deficits precisely at the Beatty jumps of $log_3 2$, no leaves, and an uncountable Cantor set of box dimension in $[0.946954, 0.949956]$. The natural conjecture that this tree is the closure of FMF growth-B patterns is _refuted_: the inclusion is strict with exponential gap (Narayana growth $1.46557$ versus $gamma >= 1.9279$). Together with the negative delimiter that the uncertified frontier grows like $1.93^n$ forever, this proves no finite-modulus certificate scheme, at any horizon, can close the pointwise gap. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 54.

#theorem(name: "Cycle Bounds at the Verification Floor; FMF Hop Identity")[
  (1) The 2025 verification floor $2^71$ @barina2025 does not extend Hercher's exclusion of $m$-cycles with $m <= 91$: the unique surviving candidate at $m = 92$ is the 43rd convergent of $log_2 3$, with $(K, L) = ($ 205632218873398596256, 120287136981023372109 $)$, whose gap $6.2447 times 10^(-43)$ lies a factor $5.1$ below the achievable width floor; $m = 92$ becomes excludable exactly when the floor reaches about $2^(73.869)$. Conditional milestone map: $2^74 arrow.r.double m <= 92$; $2^76 arrow.r.double 100$; $2^82 arrow.r.double 103$; $2^90 arrow.r.double 122$. (2) The Simons--de Weger local-minimum count $m$ of any nontrivial cycle @simons2005 equals its number of FMF hops. Hence the current record reads: every nontrivial Collatz cycle makes at least $92$ FMF hops.
]

_Proof sketch._ The Diophantine side re-runs the standard $m$-cycle machinery against the current floor; the binding obstructions are exactly the continued-fraction convergents of $log_2 3$ preceding the large partial quotients $37$ and $55$. The hop identity follows from matching the local minima of a cycle with the FMF decomposition of its odd values. Two structural complements: all-growth-B cycles are impossible; and the $3n-1$ map's $17$-cycle is an all-B two-hop cycle, so no sign-agnostic argument can force a Type A hop in a hypothetical cycle. Details: `proof.md`, Theorem 53.

== A candidate result, explicitly excluded from this paper's claims

